This repository uses three licenses. Which one applies depends on the file.

1. Text: CC BY-NC 4.0 (LICENSE-CC-BY-NC-4.0.txt)
   SKILL.md, everything in references/ and the prose in evals/ (prompts,
   case files, graders and the benchmark report).

   references/rules.md and the rule table in SKILL.md paraphrase and follow
   the rule order of Richard Rutter, "The Elements of Typographic Style
   Applied to the Web" (https://webtypography.net,
   https://github.com/clagnut/webtypography), which is published under
   CC BY-NC 4.0. That work is itself based on Robert Bringhurst, "The
   Elements of Typographic Style". Because this text adapts that material,
   it is shared under the same license. Changes: the rules are paraphrased,
   the CSS is rewritten for current browsers and measured corrections,
   logical-property, direction and locale guidance are added.

   Attribution for this repository: "Typography skill by Petri Lahdelma,
   adapting webtypography.net by Richard Rutter, CC BY-NC 4.0".

2. Code: MIT (LICENSE-MIT.txt)
   Everything in scripts/, package.json and the shell scaffolds in evals/
   (place-files.sh).

3. Fonts: SIL Open Font License 1.1
   The font files in evals/ofl-feature-check/files/fonts/ are unmodified
   copies of Source Serif 4 (Adobe) and Outfit, each shipped beside its own
   OFL text. They are test fixtures only.

4. Name and brand artwork: not licensed for reuse
   The Measured Type name, the wordmark and the artwork in .github/brand/
   identify this project. Neither license above covers them; you may use
   them to refer to this project. The wordmark lettering is outlined from
   Source Serif 4 (Adobe, OFL); the typefaces themselves remain under
   their own licenses.

skills/measured-type/LICENSE repeats sections 1 and 2 for the folder an
install copies, so the notices travel with the skill.

The NonCommercial term restricts uses primarily intended for commercial
advantage. Whether a given use, such as an agent consulting the text during
paid client work, falls under it is not settled by this note.

This note is a description of intent, not legal advice. If the
NonCommercial term matters for how you want to use the text, read the
license itself or ask the rights holders.
